  <paragraphs>
    <title>A few more comments on step functions</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Step functions let us break up the domain and avoid forcing global structure.</p></li>
        <li><p>We need to make decisions about the <m>c_i</m>. This can be somewhat arbitrary unless the data has natural breakpoints.</p></li>
        <li><p>Step functions are popular in biostatistics and epidemiology.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>



  <paragraphs>
    <title>Basis Functions Setup</title>
    <p>Polynomial and piecewise-constant regression models are special cases of a <em>basis function</em> approach.</p>
    <p>
      <md>
        y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1b_1(x_i) + \beta_2b_2(x_i)
        + \cdots + \beta_Kb_K(x_i) + \epsilon_i
      </md>
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Pick a collection of basis functions <m>b_1(X),\cdots,b_K(X)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use least squares to figure out the coefficients.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain the <m>b_i</m> for polynomial and stepwise functions.</p></li>
            <li><p>In contrast to dimension-reduction methods, here we want to add dimensions.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>There are lots of possible options for basis functions.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><p>Examples include wavelets and Fourier series.</p></li>
                <li><p>Next, we will talk about regression splines.</p></li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs> -->

  

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Regression Splines</title>
    <p>Piecewise polynomials</p>
    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
      <stack>
        <p>Fit a polynomial regression:</p>
        <p>
          <md>
            y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1x_i + \beta_2x_i^2
            + \cdots + \beta_dx_i^d + \epsilon_i
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>Let the <m>\beta_i</m> values be different at different locations in the range.</p>
      </stack>
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Points where the coefficients change are called “knots.”</p></li>
            <li><p>A piecewise cubic with no knots is just a standard cubic polynomial.</p></li>
            <li><p>More knots give a more flexible polynomial.</p></li>
            <li><p>The piecewise-constant model from the previous section is the degree-zero version of this.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Example of piecewise polynomial</title>

        <image source="figures/Fig7-3a.png" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Wage versus Age with a discontinuous piecewise cubic regression line split at age fifty.</shortdescription>
        </image>


        <p>Example:
        <md>
            y_i = \begin{cases} 
            \beta_{01} + \beta_{11}x_i + \beta_{21}x_i^2 + \beta_{31}x_i^3 + \epsilon_i \amp \text{if } x_i &lt; c \\ 
            \beta_{02} + \beta_{12}x_i + \beta_{22}x_i^2 + \beta_{32}x_i^3 + \epsilon_i \amp \text{if } x_i \geq c 
            \end{cases}
        </md>
        
        </p>        


    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>The model uses 8 degrees of freedom.</p></li>
        <li><p>The problem is that the function is discontinuous.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>The fix</title>
    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Fit a piecewise polynomial.</p></li>
            <li><p>Require continuity at the knots.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
      <stack>
        <image source="figures/Fig7-3b.png">
          <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Wage versus Age with a continuous piecewise cubic regression line joined at age fifty.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
    <p>The curve looks better, but the pointy join is odd.</p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>The better fix: Cubic splines</title>
    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Fit a piecewise polynomial.</p></li>
            <li><p>Require continuity at the knots.</p></li>
            <li><p>Require the first and second derivatives to be continuous at the knots.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Now we have smooth joins. This is called a “cubic spline.”</p></li>
            <li><p>The model has 8 original degrees of freedom, plus three constraints (continuity, first-derivative continuity, and second-derivative continuity), leaving 5 degrees of freedom.</p></li>
            <li><p>A cubic spline with <m>K</m> knots uses <m>3+K</m> degrees of freedom.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
      <stack>
        <image source="figures/Fig7-3c.png">
          <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Wage versus Age with a smooth red cubic spline regression line joined at age fifty.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p>
          Test your understanding:
          <url href="https://PollEv.com/multiple_choice_polls/bkj0zshpQHzhiwM3UXroS/respond">PollEv</url>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Example</title>
    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
      <stack>
        <p>We have the following piecewise cubic polynomial:</p>
        <p>
          <md>
            f(x) =
            \begin{cases}
            2+x+x^2+0.1x^3 \amp x \leq 1 \\
            b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2-x^3 \amp x &gt; 1
            \end{cases}
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>What are <m>b_0</m>, <m>b_1</m>, and <m>b_2</m> to make this a cubic spline?</p>
        <p>Check your answers:</p>
      </stack>
      <stack>
        <image source="figures/SplineMatchingExample.png">
          <shortdescription>Polynomial curve on a coordinate grid ending at point (1, 4.1).</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
        <figure>
          <caption>Desmos cubic spline matching example</caption>
          <interactive desmos="kbm0zivqco" />
        </figure>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>More space for work</title>
    <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <md>
            f(x) =
            \begin{cases}
            2+x+x^2+0.1x^3 \amp x \leq 1 \\
            b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2-x^3 \amp x &gt; 1
            \end{cases}
          </md>
        </p>
        <p component="slides-inst-notes">
          Instructor tool for generating the solution while varying <m>b_3</m>:
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5prmps9cuo">Desmos calculator</url>
        </p>
      </stack>
      <stack>
        <p>One solution is:</p>
        <p>
          <md>
            f(x) =
            \begin{cases}
            2+x+x^2+0.1x^3 \amp x \leq 1 \\
            3.1-2.3x+4.3x^2-x^3 \amp x &gt; 1
            \end{cases}
          </md>
        </p>
        <image source="figures/SplineMatchingExample-Soln.png">
          <shortdescription>Graph on a coordinate grid showing a solid red curve and a dashed blue curve meeting at the point (1, 4.1), representing a piecewise spline solution.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>
</handout>